  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon shows a subtropical ridge \ncentered over the bootheel of MO, with a monsoon shortwave coming \nout of Wyoming that will run around the north side of the ridge \nthrough the night. At the surface, there's a stationary front a \nlittle north of the I-80 corridor in Iowa, with dewpoints pooling up \ninto the upper 70s along it. As we go through the night, the WY \nshortwave and IA warm front will work together to support an \noverrunning convective event over southern MN. Current expectations \nare that storms will develop this afternoon in the Dakotas \nassociated with the shortwave, with elevated storms developing north \nof the warm front near the MN/IA border shortly after sunset as the \nLLJ over IA starts to ramp up (eventually getting up into the 35-40 \nkt range). We're seeing the idea from the HRRR and other CAMS that \nwe could see multiple clusters of storms develop on the nose of the \nLLJ, with the convection in the Dakotas that develops this afternoon \neventually sweeping up any clusters ahead of it late tonight. The \nfavored zone for heavy rain and severe potential will be on the nose \nof the LLJ and along the MUCAPE gradient. The HREF/REFS continue to \nshow these two features lining up along the MN/IA, with the southern \ntwo or three tiers of counties in MN being most at risk for storms, \nheavy rain, and severe weather tonight, which matches up well with \nthe Day 1 Slight Risk from the SPC. From the severe weather \nperspective, instability (muCAPE > 3000 j/kg) and deep shear (over \n40 kts) are certainly favorable for severe weather, but weak mid-\nlevel lapse rates and overnight timing/nocturnal stabilization will \nbe limiting factors that will put a ceiling on this being a \nsignificant severe weather setup.\n\nFor the rest of Wednesday, we're expected to be dry as the \natmosphere recovers from the early morning convection. We'll have \nheight rises through the day, with easterly low level winds, so we \nmay have to battle some morning stratus as the low level moisture \ngets trapped beneath a building inversion. Those easterly winds and \npotential for morning clouds will result in Wednesday being the \n\"coolest\" day of the work week, with highs generally in the low to \nmid 80s, with ever increasing dewpoints, as tropical dewpoints in \nthe 70s begin spreading back north across southern and southwest MN. \n\nGoing into Wednesday night, we'll begin to see the stationary/warm \nfront lifting back north across the area. The LLJ will not be as \nstrong as what we see Tuesday night, but WAA will still be present. \nHREF shows over 2000 j/kg of muCAPE coming up with that warm front \nand the presence of the weak WAA will give us a chance of storms, \nbut it's highly uncertain if we'll get storms to fire with our \nsubsidence and building cap. This uncertainty is why we ended up \nwith pretty broad 20-40 PoPs across the area Wednesday night as we \nawait some hopefully better clarity on where mesoscale features will \nbe.\n\nAnswering the question of where mesoscale features will be will \ncontinue to be a headache as we go through Labor Day weekend. We'll \nsee a ridge axis centered over us by Saturday, that shifts to our \nwest Sunday into early next week. That ridge will come with strong \ncapping, along with a rather uncomfortable airmass, as we see highs \nnear 90 Friday and Saturday, with dewpoints in the low to mid 70s \nsending heat index values both days up into the mid to upper 90s, \nwelcome to Augtember! That ridging will also result in capping. \nHowever, given how unstable the atmosphere is, it's not taking much \nfor convective parameterization schemes in the deterministic models \nto start firing off convection. There is lots of spread with QPF in \nthe ensembles, so the on/off, mostly low chances for PoPs makes \nsense. Again, where any weak shortwaves happen to meetup with a \nsurface boundary, chaos could ensue, but where that may happen is \nstill uncertain given the capping and weak forcing. \n\nFor Sunday into early next week, the ridge axis shifts to our west, \nwith MN/WI transitioning into northwest flow. This is why you'll \nnotice temperatures in the forecast taking a step back after \nSaturday. We'll still be placed within the northern stream and \nsusceptible to mesoscale shenanigans though. At the moment, it looks \nlike Sunday night through Monday night would be a period with \nincreased storm potential, but there's still time for this to change \nin the coming days.",
